    <h1>Christian Howard</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="prose" aria-label="Biography">
    <p class="lede">Dr. Christian Howard is a trombonist and educator based in the Twin Cities, active across orchestral, chamber, and solo settings on alto, tenor, bass trombone, and euphonium.</p>

    <h2>Teaching</h2>

    <p>Christian is Visiting Lecturer of Trombone and Euphonium at the University of Minnesota, where he leads the trombone and euphonium studio, and adjunct low brass instructor at the University of Northwestern &ndash; St. Paul.</p>

    <p>His previous students have won positions with United States Navy fleet bands and a United States Army Reserve band. Others have gone on to Eastman, Carnegie Mellon, Rice, DePaul, the Lawrence Conservatory, and the University of Minnesota. <a href="/teaching/">More about the studios and how to audition</a>.</p>

    <h2>Performance</h2>

    <p>Christian holds the second trombone chair with the Duluth Superior Symphony Orchestra and plays regularly as a substitute with the Minnesota Orchestra.</p>

    <p>He has reached the finals or semifinals of the United States Army Band &ldquo;Pershing&rsquo;s Own&rdquo; auditions in each of the last three years, on both tenor and bass trombone. He was also a semifinalist for the United States Navy Concert Band and a finalist for The Orchestra Now.</p>

    <p>He is a member of Tamalpais Brass and the Compass Rose Brass Ensemble, and has performed with Andrea Bocelli.</p>

    <p>His broader performing record includes the Chautauqua Symphony Orchestra, the Chautauqua Music School Festival Orchestra, the Northern Lights Music Festival, and LoftOpera. He has been a soloist with the University of Northwestern Symphonic Band and a guest artist at the Texas State Trombone Symposium. He has also taken part in the Third Coast Trombone Retreat, the Cleveland Trombone Seminar, and the Atlantic Brass Quintet Seminar.</p>

    <h2>Research</h2>

    <p>For his doctorate Christian transcribed three songs from Ralph Vaughan Williams&rsquo;s <i>Songs of Travel</i> for trombone &mdash; &ldquo;The Vagabond,&rdquo; &ldquo;Whither Must I Wander,&rdquo; and &ldquo;The Roadside Fire.&rdquo; The accompanying study asks what it takes to play a singer&rsquo;s line on a brass instrument: how the words shape articulation, where a singer takes time, and what survives once the text is gone. The transcriptions and the paper are finished.</p>

